# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every group below is excluded for a stated reason. An ignore entry with no
# reason attached is indistinguishable from data loss, so add the why or do not
# add the pattern.
#
# GIT DOES NOT SUPPORT TRAILING COMMENTS. A line reading
#     .atl/   # the skill-registry cache
# is not "ignore .atl/ with a comment"; it is one literal pattern whose name
# happens to contain a hash and some spaces, and it matches NOTHING. The
# directory then stays untracked-and-visible forever. Every comment in this file
# therefore sits on its OWN line above the pattern it explains.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Local runtime state ---------------------------------------------------
# Machine-specific, rewritten constantly, and worth nothing to anyone else.
# Committing any of it makes the working tree permanently dirty for every clone.
#
# .atl/   - agent tool ledger, the local skill-registry cache
# .engram/ - engram MCP local runtime config
# .pi/    - Pi agent state
.atl/
.engram/
.pi/

# .pi/agent/logs/model-debugger.log is called out on purpose, because it is the
# one file in that tree that looks committable and is not: a TIMESTAMPED log. It
# grows on every debug session and its contents are meaningless a week later, so
# a committed copy is both noise and a permanent record of nothing. It is caught
# twice over, by .pi/ above and by *.log below, on purpose.

# --- Upstream reference: somebody else's work --------------------------------
# Exactly ONE file: the maintained Pi upstream theme we diff against
# (Gentleman Programming's Gentleman-Cute, kept current for gentle-shell).
# It is kept on disk and hash-pinned in sources.json so the exclusion stays
# auditable. The other five vendored theme files were deleted from disk on
# 2026-10-15 — nothing read them — but their hashes stay pinned in
# sources.json, and the checker reports an absent excluded file as expected
# rather than as a failure. See UPSTREAM.md.
#
# One path, not a directory glob, deliberately: a glob would silently swallow
# a first-party theme dropped into upstream/ later.
upstream/pi/Gentleman-Cute.json

# --- Local orchestration state ----------------------------------------------
# odd/ holds the task-tracking documents for the agent tooling that drove this
# work. They are a record of the BUILD PROCESS, not of the result: a reader of
# this repo wants the themes, the builder and the provenance, and none of the
# three live in here. It is the same category as .atl/ above - local tooling
# state, rewritten constantly, worth nothing to a clone.
#
# The D1-D9 decision record that these files carry is not lost with them: the
# parts that describe the shipped result were written into SOURCES.md and
# sources.json, which are tracked.
odd/

# --- Lab inbox: live-test drops awaiting triage ----------------------------------
# inbox/ receives files copied from the live install dirs (tested tweaks) and
# other test material. Nothing here is source: every file is either promoted
# into the repo's real sources or deleted, and UPSTREAM.md records which.
# Ignored so a broad `git add .` can never commit the lab by accident; when a
# file must enter history as evidence, `git add -f` it explicitly.
inbox/

# --- Dependencies ----------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/

# --- Derived indexes, editor state and OS noise -----------------------------
# .codegraph/ - local code index, a derived SQLite database, machine-specific.
# Note it also carries its own nested .gitignore, so it can look ignored even
# while this line is broken. Trust check-ignore, not the absence of a `!!`.
.codegraph/
.vscode/
.idea/
*.log
*.swp
*.swo
*~
~$*
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# --- Guard: the two build inputs that MUST stay committable ----------------
# material-trabajo/vscode-themes/theme-config.js (the palette) and build.js
# (the upstream darkening chain) are what the prototype build and its validator
# are transcribed against. Exclude either and the next clone cannot reproduce a
# single assertion in this repo - and nothing would fail loudly at clone time.
#
# These negations are a GUARD, not a fix. The property that actually matters is
# that no pattern above matches either file; the negations are here so that
# someone broadening a pattern later cannot quietly break the clone. They work
# only because nothing above excludes an ANCESTOR directory: git cannot
# re-include a file whose parent directory is itself excluded.
#
# VERIFYING THIS, and a trap worth knowing about:
#   git check-ignore -v <path>   prints the LAST pattern that matched and exits 0
#                                for a NEGATED match too. So for these two files it
#                                prints the '!' line below and exits 0, which looks
#                                like "ignored" and is the opposite. A leading '!' is
#                                the only thing that distinguishes the two cases.
#   git check-ignore <path>      is the one that answers the question: prints the
#                                path and exits 0 when IGNORED, prints nothing and
#                                exits 1 when not ignored. Expect the latter, twice.
#   git status --porcelain --ignored=matching
#                                is the whole-tree view: ignored files are prefixed
#                                '!!'. Neither build input appears there.
!material-trabajo/vscode-themes/theme-config.js
!material-trabajo/vscode-themes/build.js
